{"the_sieve_result_encodes_how_many_times":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The diff adds and exports qs_factor, which returns a prime-to-multiplicity dictionary and explicitly accumulates exponents for even factors, perfect powers, repeated sieve factors, and recursively split factors. The legacy qs API remains a set wrapper for compatibility."},"a_cached_factor_agrees_with_what_recompu":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"FactorCache validates that every stored value is a prime divisor of its key, and factorint removes cached factors with remove(), accumulating their actual multiplicities. Recomputed factorizations are also fed back into the cache, so use of a cache does not change the resulting factorization."},"adjoint_behaves_as_the_conjugate_transpo":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"Matrix conjugation, transposition, and adjunction are kept distinct and related correctly. In particular, matrix products reverse order under adjoint but retain order under conjugation, while sums and Hadamard products apply the operation factorwise."},"a_series_expansion_is_produced_by_real_e":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"zeta._eval_nseries now constructs the actual Laurent expansion at s = 1 using Stieltjes constants and the pole term 1/(s - 1), then passes it through nseries with an Order term. It does not merely truncate the original expression as a fallback."},"substitution_decides_its_call_shape_by_i":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"Basic.subs uses the identity check arg2 is None to distinguish the one-argument and two-argument forms. Consequently falsy replacements such as 0 or False are still treated as a supplied second argument rather than being mistaken for the iterable form."},"assumptions_are_resolved_from_declared_f":{"score":"no","reasoning":"The new matrix assumption helper attempts to inspect explicit assumptions with conjuncts(assumptions), but conjuncts is not imported or defined in matrices.py. Generic square MatrixExpr hermitian/antihermitian queries can therefore raise NameError instead of resolving the declared fact."},"a_product_s_character_follows_from_its_f":{"score":"no","reasoning":"The Mul change improves inference when a factor is positively known to be hermitian or antihermitian, but it removes the prior unknown guard without adding a three-valued replacement. A factor for which both properties are unknown reaches the unchanged else branch and makes the product definitively False rather than unknown, so the product's character is still incorrectly assumed in unresolved cases."},"each_printer_emits_its_own_target_langua":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"Target-specific methods are supplied rather than relying on unsuitable shared output: C89 and later C printers handle fmod separately, Julia rescales SymPy's unnormalized sinc, Mathematica gets native function spellings and syntax, and dedicated cmath and PyTorch printers emit their respective APIs."},"exclusive_or_parses_by_the_language_s_ow":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The parser now maps the Python AST BitXor node directly to SymPy Xor. Since Python constructs the AST using its own precedence rules before the transformation, this preserves language precedence instead of relying on textual replacement."},"library_defined_functions_derive_from_th":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"DefinedFunction is introduced as a distinct application-only subclass, and the broad set of built-in function classes and their common bases are migrated to it. User classes based directly on Function and AppliedUndef remain distinguishable from library-defined functions."},"floor_and_ceiling_agree_with_one_another":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The shared RoundFunction logic now recognizes I times an integer even when its imaginary status is inconclusive because the integer may be zero. It also evaluates the recursive rounding operation normally, so both floor and ceiling preserve these integer-valued real and imaginary components consistently."},"each_smaller_correction_addresses_the_st":{"score":"yes","reasoning":"The miscellaneous fixes are expressed as general structural or semantic conditions—such as noncommutativity, realness, zero denominators, bound variables, target-language syntax, and polynomial-domain failures—rather than hard-coded values matching individual test inputs."}}
